The smallest unresolved case is <math>n=11</math>.  It is known that 11 unit squares cannot be packed in a square of side length less than <math>\textstyle 2+\frac{4}{\sqrt{5}} \approx 3.789</math>. By contrast, the tightest known packing of 11 squares is inside a square of side length approximately 3.877084 found by [[Walter Trump]].<ref name=survey /><ref>The 2000 version of {{harvtxt|Friedman|2009}} listed this side length as 3.8772; the tighter bound stated here is from {{citation
 | last1 = Gensane | first1 = Thierry
 | last2 = Ryckelynck | first2 = Philippe
 | year = 2005
 | title = Improved dense packings of congruent squares in a square
 | journal = [[Discrete & Computational Geometry]]
 | mr = 2140885
 | doi = 10.1007/s00454-004-1129-z
 | doi-access = free
 | volume = 34
 | issue = 1
 | pages = 97–109
}}</ref>

The smallest case where the best known packing involves squares at three different angles is <math>n=17</math>. It was discovered in 1998 by John Bidwell, an undergraduate student at the [[University of Hawaiʻi]], and has side length <math>a\approx 4.6756</math>.<ref name=survey />

