Some numbers of unit squares are never the optimal number in a packing. In particular,
if a square of size <math>a\times a</math> allows the packing of <math>n^2-2</math> unit squares, then it must be the case that <math>a\ge n</math>
and that a packing of <math>n^2</math> unit squares is also possible.<ref name="ks02">{{Cite journal |last=Nagamochi |first=Hiroshi |date=2005-07-30 |title=Packing Unit Squares in a Rectangle |url=https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v12i1r37 |journal=The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics |volume=12 |language=en |article-number=R37 |doi=10.37236/1934 |issn=1077-8926|doi-access=free }}</ref>

===Asymptotic results===
{{unsolved|mathematics|What is the asymptotic growth rate of wasted space for square packing in a half-integer square?}}

For larger values of the side length <math>a</math>, the exact number of unit squares that can pack an <math>a\times a</math> square remains unknown.
It is always possible to pack a <math>\lfloor a\rfloor \!\times\! \lfloor a\rfloor</math> grid of axis-aligned unit squares,
but this may leave a large area, approximately <math>2a(a-\lfloor a\rfloor)</math>, uncovered and wasted.<ref name="survey" />
Instead, [[Paul Erdős]] and [[Ronald Graham]] showed that for a different packing by tilted unit squares, the wasted space could be significantly reduced to <math display="inline">O(a^{7/11}) \subseteq o(a^{0.637})</math> (the latter written in [[little o notation]]).<ref>{{citation
 | last1 = Erdős | first1 = P. | author1-link = Paul Erdős
 | last2 = Graham | first2 = R. L. | author2-link = Ronald Graham
 | year = 1975
 | title = On packing squares with equal squares
 | journal = [[Journal of Combinatorial Theory]]
 | series = Series A
 | doi = 10.1016/0097-3165(75)90099-0
 | doi-access = free
 | mr = 0370368
 | volume = 19
 | pages = 119–123
 | url = http://www.math.ucsd.edu/~sbutler/ron/75_06_squares.pdf
 }}</ref>
Later, Graham and [[Fan Chung]] further reduced the wasted space to <math display="inline">O(a^{0.631})</math>,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Chung |first1=Fan |last2=Graham |first2=Ron |date=2009-08-01 |title=Packing equal squares into a large square |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0097316509000272 |journal=Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A |volume=116 |issue=6 |pages=1167–1175 |doi=10.1016/j.jcta.2009.02.005 |issn=0097-3165|url-access=subscription }}</ref> and subsequent work reduced the wasted space to <math display="inline">O(a^{0.625})</math>,<ref>{{Citation |last1=Wang |first1=Shuang |title=A New Result on Packing Unit Squares into a Large Square |date=2016-04-09 |arxiv=1603.02368 |last2=Dong |first2=Tian |last3=Li |first3=Jiamin}}</ref> and then <math display="inline">O(a^{0.6})</math>.<ref>{{Cite thesis |last=McClenagan |first=Rory |title=Asymptotic square packing problems |date=2024 |degree=Master of Science |publisher=University of Northern British Columbia |url=https://unbc.arcabc.ca/islandora/object/unbc%3A59553 |doi=10.24124/2024/59553 |page=47|url-access=subscription }}</ref>
However, as [[Klaus Roth]] and [[Bob Vaughan]] [[mathematical proof|proved]], all solutions must waste space at least <math>\Omega\bigl((a \cdot |a-\operatorname{round} a|)^{1/2}\bigr)</math>. In particular, when <math>a</math> is a [[half-integer]], the wasted space is at least proportional to its [[square root]].<ref>{{citation
 | last1 = Roth | first1 = K. F. | author1-link = Klaus Roth
 | last2 = Vaughan | first2 = R. C. | author2-link = Bob Vaughan
 | year = 1978
 | title = Inefficiency in packing squares with unit squares
 | journal = [[Journal of Combinatorial Theory]]
 | series = Series A
 | doi = 10.1016/0097-3165(78)90005-5
 | doi-access = free
 | mr = 0487806
 | volume = 24
 | issue = 2
 | pages = 170–186
}}</ref> The precise [[asymptotic analysis|asymptotic growth rate]] of the wasted space, even for half-integer side lengths, remains an [[open problem]].<ref name=Brass2005/>

== In a circle ==
'''Square packing in a circle''' is a related problem of packing ''n'' unit squares into a [[circle]] with radius as small as possible. For this problem, good solutions are known for ''n'' up to 35. Here are the minimum known solutions for up to <math>n = 12</math> (although only the cases <math>n = 1</math> and <math>n = 2</math> are known to be optimal):<ref>{{citation |last=Friedman |first=Erich |date= |title=Squares in Circles |url=https://erich-friedman.github.io/packing/squincir/}}</ref>

